or Russia's invasion of Ukraine  triggered a certain and massive  humanitarian Exodus since February more  than a million Ukrainian men women and  children have registered with  authorities here in Germany now a new  multi-year study is trying to document  what they've been through researchers  have interviewed thousands of Ukrainian  refugees who are now in Germany trying  to gain insights into the challenges of  integration things like accessing  services and finding School places and  they're documenting the psychological  impact of these experiences  well let's hear directly from someone  who's been through that process Elena  rehovah fled the war in Ukraine and now  lives at here in Berlin welcome to the  studio thank you so much for joining us  now I understand you've been here since  May three or four months uh enter the  war what prompted your decision to seek  Asylum here  um actually When The War Began me and my  friends decided to  leave to the Village nearby the cave  because we thought it would be more safe  and safer but it was a bad decision  because that Village was occupied by  Russian soldiers and who have been  caught of electricity  Heating and etc for weeks and we  couldn't escape because uh every car  which tried to Fleet it  Village was shot by Russian soldiers but  one day it was succeed to escape and we  decided that we need to  goes down go somewhere from from Ukraine  to stay safe so my friends so I decided  to  come to Germany because they were  looking for any country any family that  can accept them and they reached Julia  on one of the websites  she offered for the whole family to come  here in Berlin and basically  there are three families they live in  one house but in separate Flats they  accepted us as a real family they helped  us a lot with our papers financial help  they treat us like a family and I'm not  gonna lie that um  I will be grateful to those people to  the end of my days so they and so how  how many of you uh made it out together  oh  seven people mean seven people and so  you were in this this Village so how did  you how did you escape how did you get  past the Russians  um there was a  Green Way or how should I call it uh  when Ukrainian military forces uh  provides that oh like a corridor yes  succeeded to to escape that day right  and so you arrived in in Germany how  have you you found the process of  navigating a German bureaucracy it  sounds like you you had help yeah we had  a really great help because  um  it's really complicated to get into  papers documents and Etc once you you're  in a foreign country so our German  family I call them German family uh  helped us a lot to go through tour  grounds and Etc to get residence permits  registrations and Etc  how how difficult would that have been  if you if you hadn't had your German  family here  um  I believe more complicated because  you're doing strange forms in a  different language yeah yeah so the  first point and the second Point once  you you're fleeing your country to  escape the war you're a bit lost you  just cannot keep your brain and okay now  I should  make the first step the second step and  Etc  upside down yeah so you need somebody to  be with you and guide you navigate you  and what is that like I understand you  you worked in PR in Ukraine so one day  you're a successful businesswoman then  the next you're asking strangers in a  foreign country and a foreign language  for somewhere to live and something to  eat what is that change like  [Music]  um  actually I think a lot of ukrainians  feels this way  um  you're like creating  um  a temporary life you know you're like  okay the world is crumbling but you know  one day I will be back to Ukraine we  will build the new life better future  and Etc but now I need to think of  creating my temporary life  you know you're treated as a temporary  that's it so one day you will return  oh yeah  but  um  they cannot answer this question like  like 400 because  um  you're building your temporary life  uh in a different country and then once  the war will be finished  uh of course you want to go back to your  Homeland but  still you have your life here as well so  I think a lot of ukrainians will be  considering the way how you can live for  both countries and in your Homeland  and your second country and have you  left anyone back home in Ukraine uh yes  my parents because basically we are from  the netsk so it's the second time we're  experiencing the war and my parents they  don't want to leave Ukraine there like  if it's too much for us right to live  our but you're still in contact with  them and yes they're safe of course well  we thank you for for joining us and and  outlining so clearly what that terrible  decision is like I wish you well thank  you Elena rehover thank you thank you  for telling me  now as we mentioned a little earlier  Russia's invasion of Ukraine has  triggered a sudden and massive  humanitarian Exodus since February more  than one million Ukrainian men women and  children have registered with our  authorities here in Germany and I'm very  pleased to welcome one of the project  leaders working on a survey of the  refugees who have come over here to  Germany doctor  um Professor Dr Katarina who's director  of the federal Institute for population  research in Germany thanks so much for  joining us today here on DW now I  believe the first phase of your study is  now complete can you please tell us more  about the people coming here from  Ukraine and the specific challenges that  they are facing  Yes actually the first phase of our  study is now done and we interviewed  over 11  000 refugees coming to Germany from the  Ukraine these are mostly actually women  80 percent of all the adults are female  we have a relatively young population  the average age is 28 years of age we  have a lot of children  almost half of the women actually  traveled with their children to Germany  and another very important thing is that  the education of these people is  relatively High compared to the Ukraine  population  and moreover we also looked if the  children are in daycare if they are in  school and actually they have started  being in daycare many many me almost  everybody is in school only three  percent have uh just online  schooling with schools in the Ukraine  what about housing because I mean I know  certainly in Berlin it's very  challenging for anyone to find  accommodation here let alone if you're  coming here as a refugee so how much of  a challenge is it for refugees to find  decent accommodation here in Germany  this yes this is of course a real  challenge but we were very surprised  from our results what we actually found  is only nine percent of the refugees are  in so-called group housing and all the  others are in some kind of private  housing Arrangements either with people  and relatives or friends they knew but  the vast majority among those in private  housing really have their own apartment  so they were able to find apartments  where they could move in which is really  very very helpful also for the  integration of these families into  Germany because as I said only a really  small minorities living in these group  housings a very high number of Ukrainian  refugees has come to Germany the only  country to take in more refugees than  Germany is Poland which of course shares  a border with Ukraine can you tell us  what is it that attracts people who are  sick taking shelter to come to Germany  this is a very important question what  we found out is that most of them came  because they knew somebody already  living in Germany and moreover also the  human rights which are which we have in  Germany are another very important  explanation what the refugees told us  why they come to Germany all right  Professor Dr Katarina director of the  federal Institute for population  research in Germany thank you so much  for your time today

